.th PCX I 4/1/77
.sh NAME
pcx \*- Pascal interpreter
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd pcx
[ obj [ arg ... ] ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
This document is a quick reference to
.it pcx
usage.  The full reference for the implementation is the UNIX Pascal
``Report Appendix.''  For an introduction and more explanation
see the UNIX Pascal ``User Manual.''
.s3
.it Pcx
interprets the abstract machine code generated by
\fIpc (I)\fR.
The default file to be interpreted, if none is given, is \fIobj\fR.
Remaining arguments are available to the program using
\fIargv\fR and \fIargc\fR.
See the ``User Manual'' and ``Report Appendix'' for details.
.s3
The first word of a Pascal object file has magic number 0404 so that the shell
can recognize Pascal objects and fork 
.it pcx.
See
.it "sh (I)"
for more details.
.s3
If the program terminates abnormally,
then the cause of the abnormal termination
is given.
If the post-mortem \fB-p\fR option of
.it "pc (I)"
was used, then a control flow backtrace will be given.
With the post-mortem option enabled, the number of statements
executed and total execution time is given at normal termination.
.dt
.sh "FILES"
obj		default object file
.br
pmon.out	profile data file for pxp
.sh "SEE ALSO"
UNIX Pascal ``User Manual,'' ``Report Appendix,'' and ``PXP User Manual,''
pc (I),
pxp (I),
pcc (I),
pxref (I)
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
The following errors result before execution begins when something is amiss
with the object file.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"objfile": cannot stat\fR
.br
Most likely, \fIobjfile\fR doesn't exist.  If a pathname was given,
an unreadable directory may have been encountered.  See
.it "stat (II)."
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"objfile": cannot open\fR
.br
No read access to \fIobjfile\fR.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"objfile": file is empty\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"objfile": not Pascal object\fR
.br
\fIObjfile\fR does not have magic number 0404.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"objfile": read error\fR
.br
Shouldn't happen. (Probably due to transient external hardware 
or software failure.)
.i0
.s3
The following errors result from errors within the Pascal program itself.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBargument to chr out of range\fR
.br
Argument to \fIchr\fR must be an integer in the range 0..127.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBdiv by zero\fR
.lp +8 6
\fBmod by zero\fR
.lp +8 6
\fBfloating divide by zero\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBcall to procedure halt\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBreference through a nil pointer\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": tried to read past eof\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBnegative argument to sqrt\fR
.lp +8 6
\fBnegative argument to ln\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBsubscript out of range\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBreference to an inactive file\fR
.br
A file, other than \fIinput\fR or \fIoutput\fR, was used before it was reset
or rewritten. Sorry, the file name is not available.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBlabel not found in case\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBconstructed set argument exceeds set bounds\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBargument to argv out of range\fR
.br
First argument was not in range \fI0..argc-1\fR.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBbad i to pack(a,i,z)\fR
.lp +8 6
\fBbad i to unpack(z,a,i)\fR
.br
The \fIi\fR given would imply reference to a non-existent element
of \fIa\fR.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBvalue out of range\fR
.br
An attempt was made to give a value to a subrange variable which
exceeds the bounds of the range.  This can occur in assignments,
procedure value parameters, or in a \fIread\fR.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBassertion failed\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBstatement count limit exceeded\fR
.br
The limit on statement execution to prevent infinite looping
has been exceeded.  Note that the current limit of 500000+
statements is very generous.  Few Pascal programs should
require more than that many statements.
See the ``Report Appendix'' for details.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBruntime stack overflow\fR
.br
The program has run out of stack space probably due to excessive recursion.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBran out of memory\fR
.i0
.s3
The following errors result from interactions with the
UNIX system and can be classified as external errors.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": quota exceeded\fR
.br
The quota limitation on the directory (or a parent directory
of the directory) in which \fIfilename\fR resides has been exceeded.  This
message most often results when too much
disk storage is being used in the user's directories.  To solve the problem
the user should remove unneeded files.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": no space on device\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": system write error\fR
.br
Shouldn't happen.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBbad data found on integer read\fR
.br
An integer did not conform to the Pascal syntax, either because
it consisted of only a sign with no digits, or because an invalid character
was found before the number was encountered.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBbad data found on real read\fR
.br
A real number did not conform to the Pascal syntax.  Note that
Pascal requires digits both before and after the decimal point.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"standard input": reset, seek failed\fR
.br
The attempt to \fIreset(input)\fR using 
.it "seek (II)"
failed.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": remove, unlink failed\fR
.br
See
.it "unlink (II)."
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": cannot remove directories\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": close failed\fR
.br
The attempt to close the file at block exit failed.
Shouldn't happen.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": cannot open\fR
.br
\fIFilename\fR, used in a \fIreset\fR,
either did not exist, or sufficient
access was not available to open it for reading. See
.it "open (II)."
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": cannot create\fR
.br
\fIFilename\fR, used in a \fIrewrite\fR, could not be created using
.it "creat (II)."
Most likely causes are the file existing with no write access for this user,
or the file not existing and no write access to this directory.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fB"filename": integer number too large\fR
.br
An integer, read from
\fIfilename\fR, was greater than \fImaxint\fR
or less than \fIminint\fR.
.i0
.s3
The following ``panics'' should never occur.
They represent internal interpreter inconsistencies.
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBpanic: stack not empty between statements\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBpanic: active frame not found in goto\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBpanic: bad argument to alloc\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBpanic: unknown error\fR
.s3
.lp +8 6
\fBpanic: bad op code\fR
.i0
.s2
Bus errors and segmentation violations may also  occur in
.it pcx.
They are not trapped, and will cause a core dump.
.sh AUTHORS
Charles Haley, William Joy, and Ken Thompson
.sh "BUGS"
All variables are cleared to 0 on block entry.
.s3
The procedures \fIdispose\fR and \fIlinelimit\fR are unimplemented (ignored.)
.s3
The functions \fIcolumn\fR, \fIline\fR, and \fIpageline\fR are unimplemented.
.s3
Post-mortem traceback is not limited.  Infinite recursion which overflows
the run-time stack causes almost infinite traceback.
